<!--
  EventSchema for the `hello.received` event consumed by the active
  scouting FSM (scouting.scxml). The typed `_event.data` channel the
  scouting FSM's exit-on-first guard compares against — no script engine.

  Field:
    - exit_on_first : zenoh-pico's `__z_scout_loop(.., bool exit_on_first)`
      parameter (src/session/scout.c:29-30), carried as uint8 (0 = collect
      the whole window, non-zero = take the first Hello and stop). The
      scouting FSM guards "leave AwaitingHello" against "stay in
      AwaitingHello" on this typed field natively.

  WHY THE MODE RIDES THE EVENT RATHER THAN A DATAMODEL VARIABLE. The
  engine-free surface (`sce:kind="statechart"`, no IScriptEngine) has no
  script-evaluated datamodel to hold a per-cycle constant, and the
  scouting FSM deliberately carries no `<datamodel>` for the same reason
  its actions are no-arg. A typed `_event.data` field is the ONE channel
  a native guard can read, so the host re-states the cycle's parameter on
  every `hello.received` it raises. The value is constant for a cycle by
  construction: the drive loop reads it from `ScoutParams::exit_on_first`,
  which is captured by value when the cycle's action bundle is built.

  WHY THIS IS NOT THE FRAGMENT `more` BIT'S SHAPE. `fragment.chunk`'s
  `more` is a WIRE fact decoded from the message that triggered the event;
  `exit_on_first` is a CALLER fact about the scouting cycle. Both reach
  the guard the same way because the engine-free surface offers one
  channel, but the field is host configuration, not peer input — nothing a
  Hello sender can influence.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       name="hello_received_schema"
       sce:kind="event-schema"
       sce:event-name="hello.received">
  <datamodel>
    <data id="exit_on_first" sce:type="uint8" sce:direction="in"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
